  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 AM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nSplit flow pattern continues to occupy North America today with \nnortherly flow and embedded waves over Canada while weak subtropical \nflow remains over the mid U.S. Embedded shortwave troughing \ncurrently over Minnesota will dig and deepen this afternoon as it \nprogresses over the Southern Ohio Valley by this weekend. Subsequent \nsurface low pressure will bring rounds of showers and storms today \nthrough Saturday before moving northeast this Sunday. \n\nConditions remain mostly benign for the remainder of the forecast \nperiod as deepening shortwave troughing will drop H8 \ntemperatures into the low teens and surface temperatures into \nthe 70s this Saturday and Sunday. Aforementioned upstream broad \nmidlevel ridging over the mid/southern U.S. will strengthen and \nextend its axis through the Great Lakes Region early next week. \nThis pattern will support surface high pressure and overall \nquiet weather through midweek next week. Long-range ensembles \ndepict midlevel closed low pressure currently over the Alaskan \nBay progressing downstream and lifting across the \nAmerican/Canadian Plains by midweek next week. Chances of \nprecipitation will likely return to the forecast around the \nWednesday/Thursday timeframe, but the latest guidance continues \nto keep highest probabilities of QPF low. \n\nForecast Details:\n\nToday and Saturday: The only real active weather for the next \nseveral days remains today and Saturday, with the aforementioned \nsurface low pressure developing over the Dakotas and digging across \nthe Mid-Mississippi Valley. The main axis of precipitation expected \nto impact the CWA will be this evening/tonight as moisture extends \nnortheast into Northern Quebec. While convective-driven rainfall is \nexpected to spread across Eastern Upper, light stratiform rainfall \nwill occupy most of Northern Lower. While most of Northern Lower can \nexpect rainfall totals between a tenth to half an inch, the \nstrongest convection remaining north of the bridge will support much \nhigher totals. Model soundings across Mackinac and Chippewa Counties \ndepict long skinny CAPE, PWAT\u2019s close to 2\u201d and low cloud layer\nshear to support training storms to deliver event totals of two\nto three plus inches by Saturday night. \n\nSunday through Thursday: Subsidence aloft will be supported by the \npreviously mentioned ridging pattern this Sunday through Wednesday. \nSurface high pressure begins to build across the Midwest, with \nclimatologically cool temperatures on Sunday followed by highs \nslowly returning to near normal for late July/early August. No \nprecipitation chances are in the forecast until the end of the long-\nterm forecast as ensembles depict large-scale troughing lifting \nthrough the Central Plains midweek next week. Probabilities of QPF \nremain low in the forecast at this time, with the highest likelihood \nremaining less than a quarter inch for the Wednesday/Thursday \ntimeframe.",
